## [ISSUE] `user != null` disables conditional passkeys on normal (non-re-auth) login

**Severity:** HIGH — passkey (conditional UI) login stops working on the standard username/password and username forms.

### The Problem
```java
protected boolean isConditionalPasskeysEnabled(UserModel user) {
    return webauthnAuth != null && webauthnAuth.isPasskeysEnabled() && user != null;
}
```
Before this PR, `authenticate()` called `fillContextForm` inside the `else` branch (user not yet known), and `challenge(ctx, error, field)` only did so when `context.getUser() == null`. The intent of the PR is to *also* enable passkeys during re-authentication (user set). The new predicate *replaces* the `user == null` case with `user != null`, instead of allowing both.

### Theory / Proof
On a fresh browser login, `context.getUser()` is null at `authenticate()` (UsernamePasswordForm.java:115) and in `challenge()` after a bad username (line 137). So `fillContextForm` is never called and the template never gets `challenge`, `rpId`, `isUserIdentified` or the `enableWebAuthnConditionalUI` data it needs to start `navigator.credentials.get({mediation: "conditional"})`. `action()` still sends WebAuthn posts to `webauthnAuth.action`, but the browser never gets a server challenge (`AUTH_CHALLENGE_NOTE` is not set), so no passkey assertion can succeed.

### What happens today vs. what will break
Re-authentication with passkeys now works. First-factor passkey login, which is the main point of the PASSKEYS feature, no longer does.

### Research
Upstream Keycloak (keycloak#40193 area) uses:
```java
return webauthnAuth != null && webauthnAuth.isPasskeysEnabled()
        && (user == null || user.credentialManager().isConfiguredFor(webauthnAuth.getCredentialType()));
```
That keeps passkeys on for anonymous login and, during re-auth, turns them on only for users who actually have a passkey credential.

### Recommendation
```java
protected boolean isConditionalPasskeysEnabled(UserModel user) {
    return webauthnAuth != null && webauthnAuth.isPasskeysEnabled()
            && (user == null || user.credentialManager().isConfiguredFor(webauthnAuth.getCredentialType()));
}
```
Also add or keep a test for a first login with passkeys where no user is set yet.
